{"name":"Rhythm & Blues","hex":"#70767b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rhythm","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The variation of strong and weak elements (such as duration, accent) of sounds, notably in speech or music, over time; a beat or meter.","A specifically defined pattern of such variation."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To impart a (particular) rhythm to."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rhythm"},{"term":"blues","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of blue"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A feeling of sadness or depression.","One's particular life experience, particularly including the hardships one has faced."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blues"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rhythm & Blues (Buddy Guy album)","blocks":[{"text":"Rhythm & Blues is the 16th studio album by American blues musician Buddy Guy. It was released in July 2013 under RCA Records. Rhythm & Blues marked Guy's largest first week sales in the Soundscan Era, and was his fourth album to reach No. 1 on the Top Blues Albums Chart."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/70/RhythmandBlues.BuddyGuy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhythm_%26_Blues_(Buddy_Guy_album)","wikidata":"Q17028937"},{"title":"Rhythm & Blues (TV series)","blocks":[{"text":"Rhythm & Blues is an American sitcom television series, created by Jordan Moffet, that aired on NBC for five weeks from September 24 to October 22, 1992, with an additional leftover episode airing on February 12, 1993. The show stars Roger Kabler, Anna Maria Horsford, Ron Glass, Troy Curvey Jr., Vanessa Bell Calloway, Miguel A. Nunez, Jr., and Christopher Babers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/1/14/Rhythm_%26_Blues.jpeg/330px-Rhythm_%26_Blues.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhythm_%26_Blues_(TV_series)","wikidata":"Q7321886"},{"title":"Rhythm and blues","blocks":[{"text":"Rhythm and blues, frequently abbreviated as R&B or R'n'B, is a genre of popular music that originated within African American communities in the 1940s. The term was originally used by record companies to describe recordings marketed predominantly to African Americans, at a time when \"rocking, jazz based music ... [with a] heavy, insistent beat\" was starting to become more popular.\nIn the commercial rhythm and blues music typical of the 1950s through the 1970s, the bands usually consisted of a piano, one or two guitars, bass, drums, one or more saxophones, and sometimes background vocalists. R&B lyrical themes often encapsulate the African American history and experience of pain and the quest for freedom and joy, as well as triumphs and failures in terms of societal racism, oppression, relationships, economics, and aspirations."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhythm_and_blues","wikidata":"Q45981"},{"title":"Rhythm","blocks":[{"text":"Rhythm generally means a \"movement marked by the regulated succession of strong and weak elements, or of opposite or different conditions\". This general meaning of regular recurrence or pattern in time can apply to a wide variety of cyclical natural phenomena having a periodicity or frequency of anything from microseconds to several seconds ; to several minutes or hours, or, at the most extreme, even over many years."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhythm","wikidata":"Q170406"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Louis_Jordan%2C_New_York%2C_N.Y.%2C_ca._July_1946_%28William_P._Gottlieb_04721%29.jpg/500px-Louis_Jordan%2C_New_York%2C_N.Y.%2C_ca._July_1946_%28William_P._Gottlieb_04721%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Louis_Jordan%2C_New_York%2C_N.Y.%2C_ca._July_1946_%28William_P._Gottlieb_04721%29.jpg/500px-Louis_Jordan%2C_New_York%2C_N.Y.%2C_ca._July_1946_%28William_P._Gottlieb_04721%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Louis Jordan in New York City, c. July 1946","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALouis_Jordan%2C_New_York%2C_N.Y.%2C_ca._July_1946_(William_P._Gottlieb_04721).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Big_Joe_Turner_Rock_and_Roll_Revue_Apollo_Theater_1955.jpg/500px-Big_Joe_Turner_Rock_and_Roll_Revue_Apollo_Theater_1955.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Big_Joe_Turner_Rock_and_Roll_Revue_Apollo_Theater_1955.jpg/500px-Big_Joe_Turner_Rock_and_Roll_Revue_Apollo_Theater_1955.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Big Joe Turner was a key figure in early R&B, blending blues and rock.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABig_Joe_Turner_Rock_and_Roll_Revue_Apollo_Theater_1955.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Habanera_cut-time.jpg/500px-Habanera_cut-time.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Habanera_cut-time.jpg/1280px-Habanera_cut-time.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Habanera_cut-time.jpg/500px-Habanera_cut-time.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The habanera rhythm shown as tresillo (lower notes) with the backbeat (upper note)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHabanera_cut-time.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Dizzy_Gillespie01.JPG/500px-Dizzy_Gillespie01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content